O|
M|                                A HAUNTING IN VENICE                                
O|
M|                                       written                                      
O|
M|                                         by                                         
O|
M|                                    Michael Green                                   
O|
M|                        based on the novel “Hallowe’en Party”                       
O|
M|                                         by                                         
O|
M|                                   Agatha Christie                                  
O|
O|
O|                                                                 1.                 
O|
T|               FADE IN ON:                                                          
S|               EXT. VENICE, ITALY. PRE-DAWN.                                        
O|
N,G|               SHOTS OF THE SINKING, SEPULCHRAL CITY. CHILLED BY AUTUMN.            
N|               IN A BURIAL SHROUD OF MIST.                                          
O|
N|               The city’s secrets open to us in creeping images of crypts,          
N|               graves, suffering statues, gargoyles in pain. A stone                
N|               hunchback cowers. Hercules dares witches with his club.              
N|               Pigeons at war over scraps.                                          
O|
N|               Through the gloom and mist emerge the two bronze shepherds           
N|               atop St. Mark’s Clock. Their hammers raised --                       
O|
N|               And STRIKE the bell --                                               
O|
T|                                                                CUT TO:             
O|
S|               INT. BEDROOM. RECTORY. PRE-DAWN.                                     
O|
N|               HERCULE POIROT WAKES WITH A GASP.                                    
N|               A moment of shock. A nightmare’s duress slow to fade...              
O|
Y|               TITLE:       A HAUNTING IN VENICE                                    
O|
S|               EXT. VENICE STREETS. DAWN.                                           
O|
N|               A SEAGULL flies off a cornice GROTESQUE. MUSIC RISES: “WHEN          
N|               THE LIGHTS GO ON AGAIN (ALL OVER THE WORLD)” as --                   
O|
N,G|               We take in “La Serenissima” at its best. Picture postcard            
N|               Venice now, with only the few EARLY RISERS awake to the dawn:        
O|
N|               OLD NUNS sweep their stoop...                                        
O|
N|               YOUNG MEN wax their vaporetti...                                     
O|
N|               BLACK MARKET TRADERS sell CRATES OF EGGS from a BOAT...              
O|
Y|               TITLE CARD: VENICE, ITALY 1947.                                      
O|
S|               INT. BEDROOM. RECTORY. MORNING.                                      
O|
N,M|               Looking in a mirror, Poirot combs out his (smaller)                  
N|               moustaches, a visible touch of scar brushed over.                    
S|               EXT. RECTORY GARDEN. MORNING.                                        
O|
N|               Poirot paces outside a small stone rectory. Inspecting his           
N|               crop.  Autumn vegetables in perfect alignment. Leaves lush.          
N,G|               A view of the MAJESTIC CHURCH his rented rectory abuts.              
O|
O|
O|                                                                 2.                 
O|
N|               He kneels to brush SLUGS off the KALE to find: A SPIDER.             
N|               He carefully picks it off when -- AN ENGINE APPROACHES.              
O|
N|               Poirot lights up. Tucks the spider under A JAR and --                
O|
N|               AT THE RIVER RAILING                                                 
O|
N|               Poirot leans over the grilled gate as -- A SMALL DELIVERY            
N|               BOAT approaches. A BAKER WHISTLES to Poirot with excitement.         
N|               Stands to hand him A SPECIAL PASTRY BOX.                             
O|
C|                                   BAKER                                            
U|                         Eggs!  The eggs came, Signore!                             
O|
N|               Poirot refuses the NEWSPAPER offered with the box. Opens it          
N|               greedily.  Voila! A gorgeous assortment inside.                      
O|
N|               Poirot takes the delights with delight and --                        
O|
S|               EXT. RECTORY GATE. MORNING.                                          
O|
N|               Poirot in an impeccable coat, pastry box under elbow. He             
N|               nibbles a godsend coronetti as he opens his squeaky GATE to --       
S|               EXT. VENICE STREET.                                                  
O|
N|               -- WHERE A GROUP OF PETITIONERS WAIT FOR HIM. Like fans              
N|               camped out for concert tickets. Telegrams and letters in             
N|               hand, all CALLING his name. Eager and insistent.                     
O|
C|                                   DESPERATE PETITIONER                             
U|                         Monsieur Poirot -- you must help me. My                    
U|                         parents both mysteriously died last year,                  
U|                         then my brother soon after -- our doctor                   
U|                         can’t explain it --                                        
O|
N|               Poirot sidesteps them all, tuning it all out. Pulls the JAR          
N|               from his pocket...                                                   
O|
N|               Gently deposits the SPIDER on a wall. Bye friend.                    
O|
N|               But DESPERATE PETITIONER runs at Poirot --                           
O|
C|                                   DESPERATE PETITIONER                             
U|                         Please Monsieur, they say we’re cursed --                  
U|                         I fear for my life! --                                     
N|               Very suddenly, Desperate Petitioner is CLOTHESLINED across           
N|               the chest -- and SLAMS to the ground. REVEAL:                        
O|
N,I|               VITALE PORTFOGLIO. Gruff, powerful, protective, 50s.                 
N,I|               Retired cop come bodyguard who could stand down a Minotaur           
N,I|               with a look, as he does now to Petitioner --                         
O|
O|
O|                                                                 3.                 
O|
C|                                   VITALE PORTFOGLIO                                
U|                         What’d I tell you? Touch him again and I                   
U|                         keep the hand.                                             
P|                             (to Poirot, not the first time)                        
U|                         Dottore.  I beg you not to come out till                   
U|                         I’m on the clock.                                          
N|               Poirot walks off at a clip, Portfoglio SIGHS. Warns any              
N|               followers with a look. A CHURCH BELL STRIKES THE HOUR and --         
O|
S|               EXT. CANAL BRIDGE. MORNING.                                          
O|
N|               POIROT crosses A BRIDGE at his leisurely pace, savoring the          
N,G|               crunch of a pizzelle cookie. He crosses FRAME -- a moment --         
O|
N|               Then Portfoglio follows behind him. He’s got a cookie too.           
O|
N,G|               ON PORTFOGLIO. His head turns sharply, sensing -- ANOTHER            
N|               PETITIONER -- RUNNING at Poirot --                                   
O|
C|                                   ANOTHER PETITIONER                               
U|                         Signore Poirot, I need to speak with you                   
U|                         about my case, please!                                     
O|
N|               Portfoglio’s ELBOW sends him SPLASHING into the canal, and --        
S|               EXT. ISLAND OF SAN GIORGIO MAGGIORE. MORNING.                        
O|
N|               Poirot steps off a boat, nibbling another pastry, moving for--       
O|
N|               A GROCER’S BOAT. An OLDER WOMAN swoons at the offering of            
N|               FRESH EGGS from THE GROCER. Poirot holds up TWO FINGERS to           
N|               Grocer -- who in return offers him TWO EGGS OF DIFFERENT             
N|               SHAPE, COLOR AND SIZE. Decidedly unequal and asymmetrical.           
O|
N|               Poirot considers them. Measures them. Briefly squeamish.             
O|
N|               Then nods happily, hands over his cash, and --                       
O|
S|               EXT. MUSEUM STEPS. DOGE’S PALACE. MORNING.                           
O|
N|               Poirot and Portfoglio walk up to the closed palace doors. A          
N|               GUARD at attention, a tip of his cap, as --                          
C|                                   VITALE PORTFOGLIO                                
U|                         I wonder if you only hired me because I                    
U|                         can get you in before the tourists.                        
O|
N|               Poirot thanks Guard with the pastry box.                             
O|
N,G|               CLOSE ON:  POIROT’S HAND. A TREMOR.                                  
O|
N|               Poirot balls his hand into a fist. Steadies himself. Steps           
N|               inside.  Guard SHUTS the doors behind him --                         
O|
O|
O|                                                                 4.                 
O|
S|               INT. MUSEUM. DOGE’S PALACE. MORNING.                                 
N,G|               ON POIROT. The museum to himself.  Alone. In front of --             
O|
N|               Tintoretto’s “Paradise.” Massive, messy. Poirot takes it             
N|               in.  Challenging peace before chaos.                                 
O|
N|               Breathe.                                                             
O|
S|               EXT. RECTORY GARDEN. DAY.                                            
O|
N|               A lunch of TWO UNEVEN EGGS wait in their cups.                       
O|
N,G|               ON POIROT. Alongside huge MARROWS, PUMPKINS. Pleased with            
N|               them, as --                                                          
O|
N|               Portfoglio emerges from the rectory, reluctant to intrude.           
C|                                   VITALE PORTFOGLIO                                
U|                         Monsieur Poirot?... Dottore --                             
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         What is the word for a marrow this grand?                  
U|                         A voluptuous vegetable.                                    
O|
C|                                   VITALE PORTFOGLIO                                
U|                         There’s a lady here. She says she’s in                     
U|                         Venice on urgent business. She says                        
U|                         she’s a friend --                                          
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         I don’t have any friends.                                  
O|
C|                                   VITALE PORTFOGLIO                                
U|                         She said you would say that. So I should                   
U|                         give you this...                                           
O|
N|               Vitale produces... AN APPLE.  Poirot instantly brightens.            
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         The Authoress! Mon vielle!                                 
O|
S|               EXT. RECTORY GATE. DAY.                                              
N|               The door opens on --                                                 
O|
N,I|               ARIADNE OLIVER. An ever-busy mind in an even busier body.            
N|               An instant sense that no matter what life handed her she’d           
N|               become precisely this mile-a-minute intellectual lighthouse          
N|               we meet in tweed right here.                                         
O|
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         Hello, young man. Is your mother at                        
U|                         home?                                                      
O|
O|
O|                                                                 5.                 
O|
S|               EXT. ROOF TERRACE. OVERLOOKING VENICE. DAY.                          
N|               Portfoglio comes up the steps with A FRESH PASTRY BOX.               
N|               Carrying it to... A TERRACE.  360-Venice-views from a high           
N|               vantage.  He sets down the box, sparing a glare for Ariadne.         
O|
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         He’s safe with me, I only murder people                    
U|                         in books. Vamoosh.                                         
O|
N|               Portfoglio leaves. His eyebrows argue unhappily.                     
O|
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         That man. Really.                                          
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         You must forgive my bodyguard, his                         
U|                         instructions are mine. No one may pass                     
U|                         but the pastry man twice a day.                            
O|
N|               He offers an enticing Zuccotto. Ariadne declines, taking an          
N|               fresh apple from her bag of them and biting in.                      
O|
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         You know me, apples only till supper.                      
U|                         It’s my besetting sin. You go ahead.                       
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         Well, I haven’t had a thing all day...                     
U|                         Perhaps a little taste.                                    
O|
N|               He helps himself. Ariadne takes a moment, takes it all in.           
O|
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         Dog my cats, it’s true. Hercule Poirot                     
U|                         really has gone silent. Walled himself                     
U|                         up into retirement. Cakes for cases.                       
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         I am much satisfied.                                       
O|
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         No.  This is happiness not satisfaction.                   
U|                         A writer knows the difference. Even                        
U|                         picked Venice to hide in, a gorgeous                       
U|                         relic slowly sinking into the sea, just                    
U|                         like your mind without a challenge -- ha!                  
U|                         -- don’t underestimate me for a clever                     
U|                         turn of phrase -- I am the world number                    
U|                         one mystery writer. Or was anyway.  Best                   
U|                         sellers 27 of 30 books, damn the critics                   
U|                         on the last three. Called them all                         
U|                         “Small beer.”                                              
O|
O|
O|                                                                 6.                 
O|
N|               A CRUNCH of apple to punctuate. Poirot enjoys her assault.           
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         Ariadne Oliver... it is good to see you.                   
U|                         Come by way of Spain.                                      
P|                             (off her look)                                         
U|                         Your apples. Manzana Reineta del Bierzo,                   
U|                         a Spanish cultivar.                                        
O|
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         This is what’s come of the great sleuth?                   
U|                         Gardening tricks. Poirot postmortem,                       
U|                         cause of death: Routine.  What about                       
U|                         your little grey cells? What about the                     
U|                         innocents? Where do they find justice?                     
O|
N|               Poirot’s light air darkens. Cake turned to ash in his mouth.         
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         There is no justice. The innocent are                      
U|                         dead.                                                      
O|
N|               A pause.  Ariadne briefly brought low... Then standing               
N|               suddenly.  Determined to quash his fatalism.                         
O|
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         Well then. Only one thing to do about                      
U|                         it:  You’re coming with me. Time to                        
U|                         bring some life back into your life.                       
N|               Poirot meets her eye. A defiant bite of cake.                        
O|
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         Didn’t you hear me, this is urgent!                        
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         You are not the first who has come to                      
U|                         seduce me with some irresistible case.                     
O|
N|               Ariadne’s eyes sparkle with mystery. Hook baited.                    
O|
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         Not a case. Much spiffier than that.                       
U|                         You really are cut off from the world,                     
U|                         don’t you know what today is?                              
O|
S|               EXT. CHURCH. DAY.                                                    
O|
N|               A WHISTLE -- A CRACK! FIREWORKS EXPLODE low in the sky. A            
N|               halo of SPARKS plumes and rains down against a church facade.        
O|
O|
O|                                                                 7.                 
O|
S|               EXT. BRIDGE. VENICE. DAY.                                            
O|
N,G|               CLOSE ON:  A CHILD IN A HALLOWEEN COSTUME. Catching CANDY            
N|               also raining down from the sky.                                      
O|
N|               OTHER KIDS, ALL IN MASKS, LAUGHING. REVEAL:                          
O|
N|               A RAGAMUFFIN PARADE                                                  
O|
N|               CHILDREN MARCH OVER THE LONG BRIDGE. Chaperoned by NUNS.             
N|               AMERICAN G.I.s hand out treats, candy, prizes. CHEERING and          
N|               livening up the carnival atmosphere.                                 
O|
M|               (NOTE:  The children’s Hallowe’en costumes are in the                
M|               disconcerting homespun style of the time. More murdery than          
M|               cute.  HOBOS, GHOULS, MONSTERS. Some wear classic VENETIAN           
M|               CARNIVAL MASKS, beautiful yet sinister.)                             
N|               POIROT AND ARIADNE                                                   
O|
N|               Enjoy the spectacle. Portfoglio near.                                
O|
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         We Americans imported loud music and                       
U|                         terrible chocolate. But we also brought                    
U|                         back Hallowe’en.                                           
O|
N|               She toys with A MASK over her face. Hands it to a KID.               
O|
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         War makes too many orphans. Most never                     
U|                         saw candy before. Just wait till                           
U|                         tonight.  There’s a party just for them.                   
S|               EXT. ON THE CAMPO DE MIRACOLI. DAY.                                  
O|
N,G|               FIND POIROT, ARIADNE, PORTFOGLIO                                     
O|
N|               Watching the giddy, candy-gorging KIDS float by on GONDOLAS.         
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         You are up to something, my friend.                        
O|
N,G|               ON ARIADNE. A pause.  A confession.                                  
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         Poirot, I found something -- someone -- I                  
U|                         can’t explain. I’ve looked at it every                     
U|                         which way and can’t figure it out.                         
O|
N|               Poirot is listening.                                                 
O|
O|
O|                                                                 8.                 
O|
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         “The Unholy Ms. Reynolds.” She’s a                         
U|                         spiritualist. A medium.  According to                      
U|                         the papers anyway.                                         
O|
N|               Ariadne produces A LURID ENGLISH NEWSPAPER CLIPPING: “THE            
N|               UNHOLY MS. REYNOLDS SEES FREEDOM IN HER FUTURE”.                     
O|
N|               Poirot has stopped listening.                                        
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         A spiritist? Some telepath palm reader?                    
O|
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         Joyce Reynolds, recently released from                     
U|                         jail, was the last woman on record                         
U|                         imprisoned under the terms of the                          
U|                         Witchcraft Act of 1735. Judge called her                   
U|                         a “bedevilled enchantress owed the                         
U|                         strictest punishments of Leviticus.”                       
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         Cette pauvre enfant, we can go back to my                  
U|                         garden right now. Every crystal gazer or                   
U|                         clairvoyant is by default a fraud.                         
O|
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         You think I don’t know that? In my book                    
U|                         seances rank by Communion for bilge --                     
P|                             (catches a NUN’s eye and not flinching)                
U|                         I’ve seen a million of these so-called                     
U|                         psychics, each one a flimflam fake.                        
U|                         Then...  there’s that one. Astonishing.                    
U|                         I tell you, this Ms. Reynolds, I sat at a                  
U|                         seance... Things happened --                               
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         -- Tricks -- filaments --                                  
O|
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         I am the smartest person I ever met and I                  
U|                         can’t figure it out. So I came to the                      
U|                         second.  I need Detective Poirot to pop                    
U|                         this balloon or god help me I will end up                  
U|                         a believer. If she’s real she’s my next                    
U|                         book.                                                      
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         She is not.                                                
O|
N|               Ariadne is on the move again, heading toward the canal.              
O|
O|
O|                                                                 9.                 
O|
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         Prove it. Spot the con I can’t.  Come                      
U|                         with me to the orphans’ Hallowe’en party.                  
U|                         Then after we’re invited to a seance.                      
O|
N,G|               HOLD ON POIROT. Watching Ariadne go.  Watching AMERICAN              
N|               SOLDIERS playfully help the ORPHANS onto gondolas -- and --          
O|
S|               EXT. GONDOLA. ON THE GRAND CANAL. SUNSET.                            
O|
N|               Poirot, Ariadne, Portfoglio EACH ON THEIR OWN GONDOLA.               
O|
N|               They cut through the wispy MIST that hovers inches over the          
N|               water.  They ride silently along the Grand Canal. Imposing           
N|               palazzos on either side.                                             
S|               EXT. GONDOLA. ON THE OPEN WATER. SUNSET.                             
O|
N|               Their gondolas out on open water, the safety of the church           
N|               domes behind them. FIREWORKS light up the sky.                       
O|
S|               EXT. GONDOLA. NARROW CANAL. SUNSET.                                  
O|
N|               Their gondolas ROUND A CORNER... A BRIDGE OVERHEAD                   
N|               momentarily shrouds them in darkness, blocks their view...           
O|
N,G|               Until they clear it -- and we have our first view of --              
O|
S|               EXT. PALAZZO LACRIME DI GIOVANI.                                     
O|
N|               An august PALAZZO in early decay. Strangely brutalist.               
N|               Beautiful, dreadful. A BALCONY juts over the CAVANA                  
N|               ENTRANCE:  THE UPPER LOGGIA.                                         
O|
N|               Above it the CLOUDS thicken and lower and churn.                     
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         There it is.                                               
O|
N|               Portfoglio, as one should, reacts.                                   
O|
C|                                   VITALE PORTFOGLIO                                
U|                         Here?                                                      
O|
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         You know the place?                                        
O|
C|                                   VITALE PORTFOGLIO                                
U|                         Palazzo Lacrime di Giovani. In Venice we                   
U|                         say every house is haunted. Or cursed.                     
O|
N|               The wind picks up. RAIN begins to fall, speckling the canal.         
O|
N|               Ahead, a gondola deposits its cargo of children onto --              
O|
O|
O|                                                                 10.                
O|
S|               INT. BOATHOUSE / CAVANA. PALAZZO. DAY.                               
N|               A wide dock with room for several boats. CHILDREN step off           
N|               their gondolas, aided by the NUNS.                                   
O|
S|               INT. BOATHOUSE / CAVANA. PALAZZO. MOMENTS LATER.                     
O|
N|               Torch-lit DARKNESS. The large DOUBLE DOORS SHUT. CHILDREN            
N|               and NUNS crowded in. No sound but the pelting RAIN outside.          
O|
N|               ARIADNE AND POIROT                                                   
O|
N|               Poirot amid the excited children. Then catching the eye of --        
O|
N,I|               A BOY (LEOPOLD FERRIER) in a dark velvet MORETTA MASK.               
N|               Staring at him. The mask mouthless, mute. The boy nods               
N|               presciently in the direction of the DOORS, just as --                
O|
N|               DOORS SLAM! FLOODLIGHTS SNAP ON!  ILLUMINATING --                    
O|
N|               A CURTAIN                                                            
O|
C,E|                                   MAN’S VOICE (V.O.)                               
U|                         Are you ready, children?                                   
N|               The CHILDREN CHEER. An AMERICAN G.I. in A DEATH MASK steps           
N|               forward, weaves the yarn --                                          
O|
C|                                   DEATH MASK                                       
U|                         Welcome on this All Hallows’ Eve. The                      
U|                         Samhain. When the dead, they say, are                      
U|                         close -- and we become friends with fear.                  
U|                         Please watch your step. This house is                      
U|                         built on angry spirits and old bones.                      
U|                         Long ago this palazzo was an orphanage...                  
O|
N|               HANDS MANIPULATE SHADOW PUPPETS. THE STORY COMES TO LIFE ON          
N|               THE CURTAIN SCREEN:                                                  
O|
N|               -- IN AN ORPHANAGE. A DOCTOR PUPPET AND A NURSE PUPPET...            
N|               FOLLOWED BY A TROOP OF CHILDREN PUPPETS...                           
N|               -- THE CHILDREN ALL LINE UP FOR THEIR BEDS... CLIMBING IN...         
O|
C,E|                                   DEATH MASK (V.O.)                                
U|                         Good doctors and good nurses took good                     
U|                         care of good children. Until...                            
O|
N|               -- THE BACKGROUND INVERTS TO BECOME...                               
O|
N|               -- THE STREET OUTSIDE! THE PALAZZO AND ITS OMINOUS BALCONY           
N|               STILL CENTERED, TOWNSPEOPLE PUPPETS BUSY BELOW IT. UNTIL...          
O|
O|
O|                                                                 11.                
O|
N|               -- A BAKER PUPPET SUDDENLY TWISTS... STAGE MAGIC TRANSFORMS          
N|               HIS CLOTHES TO BLACK AND... HE DIES!                                 
N|               THE ORPHANS WATCHING THE PUPPET SHOW GASP.                           
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         It is not too frightening for the                          
U|                         children?                                                  
O|
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         Scary stories make life less scary.                        
O|
N|               -- A PLAGUE DOCTOR PUPPET STEPS ON. THE TOWNSPEOPLE BEGIN TO         
N|               SCATTER... AND DIE!  THE BACKDROP INVERTS AGAIN AND...               
O|
C,E|                                   DEATH MASK (V.O.)                                
U|                         Plagues make people afraid. And fear                       
U|                         makes good people do terrible things...                    
N|               -- BACK INSIDE THE ORPHANAGE: DOCTOR AND NURSE TIPTOE FROM           
N|               THE SLEEPING CHILDREN... LOCKING THE DOOR BEHIND THEM...             
O|
C,E|                                   DEATH MASK (V.O.)                                
U|                         The children soon realized they were                       
U|                         alone.  Locked in this house to die.                       
O|
N|               -- A CHILD PUPPET RUBS HER SLEEPY LITTLE EYES AWAKE... SHE           
N|               TODDLES TO THE BIG DOOR... TO FIND IT LOCKED...                      
O|
N|               -- THE CHILDREN ALL JOIN, PRESSING AGAINST THE LOCKED DOOR...        
O|
C,E|                                   DEATH MASK (V.O.)                                
U|                         Starving. Calling.  Clawing.                               
N|               -- THE CHILDREN, VISIBLE IN THE WINDOWS, CRY FOR HELP!               
O|
C,E|                                   DEATH MASK (V.O.)                                
U|                         Some say the children are still hiding in                  
U|                         this palazzo... And they want more                         
U|                         children to join them.                                     
O|
N|               THE ORPHANS enjoy the fright.                                        
O|
N|               Death Mask steps toward them.                                        
O|
C|                                   DEATH MASK                                       
U|                         So you be careful. For they want revenge                   
U|                         on the doctors and nurses who left them                    
U|                         here to die. Watch out for the mark                        
U|                         of...  The Children’s Vendetta.                            
