S|               INT. ANTIQUARIUM. PALAZZO. NIGHT.                                    
O|
N,G|               ON REYNOLDS. Ferrier cleans the fresh CUT on her cheek.              
N|               Checks her pupils, satisfied. He leaves her, as --                   
N|               Poirot crosses, coat on to leave. Reynolds can’t help it --          
O|
O|
O|                                                                 35.                
O|
C|                                   MS. REYNOLDS                                     
U|                         I hope you’ll come back tomorrow. Rowena                   
U|                         made me promise to sit for her again.                      
U|                         Ms. Oliver says I should prepare to be                     
U|                         quite famous.                                              
N|               Portfoglio from the balcony.                                         
O|
C|                                   VITALE PORTFOGLIO                                
U|                         Dottore, I’ll find us a boat.                              
O|
N|               A disapproving glance at Reynolds as he leaves them.                 
N|               Reynolds leans against the wall wearily.                             
O|
C|                                   MS. REYNOLDS                                     
U|                         These spirits are particularly savage.                     
U|                         Sittings always exact a price.                             
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         As I am sure do you.                                       
O|
N,G|               ON POIROT. Studying her.                                             
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         Hardly “medium.” Well done I should say,                   
U|                         perhaps even rare.                                         
C|                                   MS. REYNOLDS                                     
U|                         So I’m a steak.                                            
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         You are, amongst frauds, the gifted one.                   
O|
C|                                   MS. REYNOLDS                                     
U|                         I wish I was a fraud. Be less painful.                     
U|                         You know something of this I think,                        
U|                         Monsieur. Someone dies and we comfort                      
U|                         the grieving with secrets plain to us.                     
U|                         Both creatures who speak for the dead.                     
U|                         Who know the dead too well, I think.                       
O|
N|               For a moment her eyes go soft. Memory of real pain.                  
O|
C|                                   MS. REYNOLDS                                     
U|                         Imagine. A war nurse who hears ghosts...                   
U|                         Surrounded by screaming. On her ward.                      
U|                         Inside her mind. Wave after wave of the                    
U|                         dying and the dead. And the only thing                     
U|                         that stopped the pain was telling the                      
U|                         mourners what I heard. Ease their                          
U|                         suffering, as only I can. You’d begrudge                   
U|                         that?                                                      
N|               Poirot considers her. Her suffering.  But is still unmoved --        
O|
O|
O|                                                                 36.                
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         You made a mother believe her daughter’s                   
U|                         soul is in torment. This is not                            
U|                         generous, not gentle, not humble.                          
C|                                   MS. REYNOLDS                                     
U|                         I felt pain. I saw a murder --                             
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         Did you see who killed Alicia Drake?                       
O|
C|                                   MS. REYNOLDS                                     
U|                         It wasn’t revealed. Maybe tomorrow.                        
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         A lucrative convenience.                                   
O|
C|                                   MS. REYNOLDS                                     
U|                         Why is it so hard for you to believe --                    
U|                         death isn’t an end but a door -- and God                   
U|                         left some the key.                                         
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         Terrors for children, Ms. Reynolds.                        
O|
C|                                   MS. REYNOLDS                                     
U|                         You might learn from them. Children can                    
U|                         suffer as much as those orphans... and                     
U|                         still laugh and play and bob for apples.                   
U|                         They’re alive.                                             
N|               Reynolds eyes unfix, dissociated. Truth seen at a distance.          
O|
C|                                   MS. REYNOLDS                                     
U|                         But you... suffered and became a ghost.                    
U|                         Death everywhere you went, all your life.                  
U|                         Soldiers. Friends...  Katherine.                           
O|
N|               Poirot goes cold. A pause.                                           
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         When I meet you next it will be to tell                    
U|                         you how you do your tricks.                                
O|
C|                                   MS. REYNOLDS                                     
U|                         Then we shall not meet again.                              
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         You must persist that you are real?                        
O|
N|               Reynolds stops. Turns.  For a moment unmasked, light,                
N|               playful.  All artifice gone.                                         
O|
O|
O|                                                                 37.                
O|
C|                                   MS. REYNOLDS                                     
U|                         And if I’m not, who’s getting hurt?                        
U|                         Magic won’t come unless you call it in.                    
P|                             (quiet again, “Ms. Reynolds” again)                    
U|                         Unless it really is all true.                              
N|               Reynolds twirls HER PIERROT MASK with a grin. Puts her mask          
N|               ON POIROT’S FACE. Her cloak over his shoulders.                      
O|
C|                                   MS. REYNOLDS                                     
U|                         Lighten up, pal. You might have fun.                       
O|
N|               She smiles. Pleased with the effect.  Poirot in a Pierrot.           
O|
C|                                   MS. REYNOLDS                                     
U|                         I’d say to remember me. But you will.                      
O|
N|               And she’s gone. Poirot’s eyes, through the mask, follow              
N|               Reynolds as she crosses behind the statue -- and --                  
O|
S|               INT. PIANO NOBILE. PALAZZO. NIGHT.                                   
O|
N|               The grandfather CLOCK STRIKES MIDNIGHT, as --                        
O|
S|               INT. PALAZZO. NIGHT.                                                 
O|
N,G|               WIDE ON POIROT. Stepping through.  Cloaked and masked.               
N|               VOICES from the others upstairs fade away.                           
S|               INT. PIANO NOBILE. PALAZZO. NIGHT.                                   
O|
N,G|               HIGH WIDE ON POIROT. He crosses the empty space.                     
O|
N|               Passes the broken wreck of chandelier.                               
O|
N|               Passes the once MUSICAL CHAIRS. When --                              
O|
N|               He hears a low SUSURRATION. Like a whisper in his ear.               
O|
N|               Stops.  Turns to look --                                             
O|
N|               No one there. Only now he sees he has stopped just                   
N|               outside... THE LIBRARY.                                              
O|
N|               THE BOBBING FOR APPLES BASIN INSIDE. STILL FULL.                     
O|
N,G|               ON POIROT’S EYES. A twinkling caprice. Silly.                        
O|
S|               INT. LIBRARY. PALAZZO. CONTINUING.                                   
O|
N,G|               ON THE FIREPLACE. A log collapses.                                   
N|               Poirot steps to the BASIN. The floating apples. He checks.           
O|
O|
O|                                                                 38.                
O|
N|               No one there to see him. He lifts up his mask.                       
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         “Lighten up.”                                              
O|
N,G|               ON POIROT. He grins.  Kneels.  Hands behind his back.                
O|
N|               He THRUSTS his face into the water. Splash. Bubbles.                 
O|
N|               Wait.                                                                
O|
N|               GASP.  Poirot comes up wet. Mouth empty. Laughing.                   
O|
N|               A breath to go down again --                                         
O|
N|               A BLUR OF MOVEMENT BEHIND HIM --                                     
O|
N|               POIROT STRUCK ON THE BACK OF THE HEAD!                               
O|
N,G|               INSIDE THE BASIN, LOOKING UP: HE HITS THE WATER. Unmoving.           
N|               Surrounded by apples. A ribbon of BLOOD plumes off his NECK.         
O|
N|               Wait.  Wait.                                                         
O|
N|               He comes to. Eyes fluttering open.                                   
O|
N|               SUDDENLY PRESSED DOWN IN THE WATER                                   
N|               Poirot fights violently -- but is held firmly --                     
O|
N|               DROWNING.  Weakening -- the fight slipping --                        
O|
N|               The world going dark at the edges --                                 
O|
N|               GOES BLACK.  Stays black. Wait.                                      
O|
S|               INT. LIBRARY. PALAZZO. MOMENTS LATER.                                
O|
N|               GASP!  Poirot shocks back to consciousness with a violent            
N|               gulp of air. PORTFOGLIO hits his back. Helping him cough             
N|               out the fluid in his lungs.                                          
O|
C|                                   VITALE PORTFOGLIO                                
U|                         Monsieur Poirot! Dottore!  Can you hear                    
U|                         me?                                                        
O|
N|               Poirot nods. BLEEDING CUTS OVER HIS BACK. LIKE CLAW MARKS.           
O|
C|                                   VITALE PORTFOGLIO                                
U|                         Who did this?                                              
O|
O|
O|                                                                 39.                
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
P|                             (tries to recall)                                      
U|                         I left Ms. Reynolds... Stopped at the                      
U|                         apples -- silly. I lifted my mask --                       
P|                             (realizing)                                            
U|                         It was her mask --                                         
O|
N,G|               ON THE PIERROT MASK. IN A POOL OF WATER.                             
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         Where is Ms. Reynolds?                                     
O|
N|               A SHRIEK OF TERROR ECHOES THROUGH THE HOUSE!                         
N|               CUT ACROSS THE PALAZZO. ALL HEAR THE VIOLENT SCREAM:                 
O|
N|               LEOPOLD AND DOCTOR FERRIER IN THE KITCHEN -- NEAR NICHOLAS           
N|               AND DESDEMONA IN THE PANTRY -- ROWENA DRAKE AND OLGA SEMINOFF        
N|               RUSH OUT OF THE MUSIC ROOM -- MAXIME GERARD PAUSES WASHING           
N|               HIS HANDS IN THE BATHROOM --                                         
O|
S|               INT. ANTIQUARIUM. PALAZZO. THAT MOMENT.                              
O|
N,G|               ON ARIADNE. THE ONE SCREAMING.  STARING AT:                          
O|
N|               REYNOLDS IMPALED ON THE STATUE OF DEMETER                            
O|
N|               THE STATUE’S OUTSTRETCHED ARM PROTRUDING THROUGH REYNOLDS’           
N|               STOMACH... REACHING UP TO THE BALCONY FROM WHERE SHE FELL...         
N|               BLOOD LIKE A BALL GOWN DRAPED OVER THE MARBLE GODDESS.               
N|               SCRATCHES RAKED ACROSS REYNOLDS’ BACK. LIKE CLAW MARKS.              
O|
S|               EXT. CAMPO. PALAZZO LACRIME DI GIOVANI. NIGHT.                       
O|
N|               The canal waters rush with the onset of STORM. A LOOSE               
N|               SUPPORT PYLON BASHES its neighbor with each WAVE.                    
O|
S|               INT. MUSIC ROOM. PALAZZO. NIGHT.                                     
O|
N,G|               CLOSE ON:  THE EARTH. It OPENS at the equator. A hinged              
N|               GLOBE.  A TELEPHONE INSIDE. PORTFOGLIO opens it to dial --           
O|
C|                                   VITALE PORTFOGLIO                                
U|                         I’ll phone my old station. I should know                   
U|                         the men on duty.                                           
N|               Ariadne enters with a tea tray. Takes it to the couch, where         
N|               Poirot recovers, handkerchief to his head. Rowena notices.           
O|
C|                                   ROWENA DRAKE                                     
U|                         I thought my cupboards were all bare.                      
O|
O|
O|                                                                 40.                
O|
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         I scrounged up some tea left from the                      
U|                         party.  Found your honey in the linen                      
U|                         closet.                                                    
O|
N|               She stirs in honey. Puts the cup into Poirot’s hand. He              
N|               sips.  Grateful.                                                     
O|
N,G|               WITH NICHOLAS AND DESDEMONA. Scared.  Quietly aside --               
O|
C|                                   DESDEMONA                                        
U|                         What do we do? She was our ticket.                         
O|
C|                                   NICHOLAS                                         
U|                         We’ll get there without her, I promise.                    
N|               POIROT WATCHES THE OTHER ATTENDEES. All reeling, in shock --         
O|
C|                                   DOCTOR FERRIER                                   
U|                         None of you saw anyone on the stairs?                      
O|
C|                                   LEOPOLD                                          
U|                         Some things can’t be seen.                                 
O|
C|                                   MAXIME GERARD                                    
U|                         Maybe she jumped, she seemed the type --                   
O|
C|                                   NICHOLAS                                         
U|                         Not Ms. Reynolds, never --                                 
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         She talked about a murder. She could                       
U|                         have known something --                                    
O|
C|                                   MAXIME GERARD                                    
U|                         You still think she’s real? Please, she                    
U|                         made up that murder idea to impress a                      
U|                         famous author -- and to bait the hook on                   
U|                         a new income stream --                                     
O|
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         Then why is she dead?                                      
C|                                   MAXIME GERARD                                    
U|                         Gravity.                                                   
O|
N|               Rowena burns at Maxime accusingly.                                   
O|
C|                                   ROWENA DRAKE                                     
U|                         She spoke in Alicia’s voice. Whoever did                   
U|                         this took my last hope of ever hearing                     
U|                         her again...                                               
O|
O|
O|                                                                 41.                
O|
C|                                   MAXIME GERARD                                    
U|                         Don’t look at me, I was being                              
U|                         interrogated by your lady writer, ask                      
U|                         her.  Then ask the doctor where he was.                    
P|                             (off Ferrier)                                          
U|                         Sick bastard already killed once here --                   
O|
C|                                   OLGA SEMINOFF                                    
U|                         It wasn’t either of them. You know what                    
U|                         it was.  Hateful things live in this                       
U|                         house.  That woman called to them. And                     
U|                         they answered.                                             
O|
N,G|               ON NICHOLAS. Realizing.                                              
O|
C|                                   NICHOLAS                                         
U|                         In the war. Before she did sittings...                     
O|
N,G|               ON DESDEMONA. It hits her with a chill.                              
C|                                   DESDEMONA                                        
U|                         Ms. Reynolds was in the British army.                      
U|                         She served with the QAIMNS at Malta. She                   
U|                         was a nurse.                                               
O|
C|                                   ROWENA DRAKE                                     
U|                         A nurse.  The Children’s Vendetta.                         
O|
N|               Silence.                                                             
O|
N,G|               ON POIROT. Eyes coming to life.  The moustaches twitch.              
O|
N,G|               ON PORTFOGLIO. At the globe TELEPHONE. He hangs up.                  
O|
C|                                   VITALE PORTFOGLIO                                
U|                         The canals aren’t safe. We can’t get a                     
U|                         boat here until the storm clears.                          
O|
C|                                   ROWENA DRAKE                                     
U|                         When will that be?                                         
O|
C|                                   VITALE PORTFOGLIO                                
U|                         It’s out of police control, Signora.                       
C|                                   OLGA SEMINOFF                                    
U|                         Well I won’t sit here and wait. I never                    
U|                         spent the night in this house before and                   
U|                         I won’t now. None of you should --                         
O|
N|               Seminoff storms off --                                               
O|
S|               INT. FOYER. PALAZZO.                                                 
O|
N|               -- For the doors. The others funnel behind when --                   
O|
O|
O|                                                                 42.                
O|
N|               SEMINOFF HALTS. SURPRISED AT:                                        
N|               POIROT LOCKING THE FOYER GATES. JUST LIKE THE DOCTOR PUPPET.         
O|
N|               HE EYES EACH OF THE ATTENDEES. NOW ALL SUSPECTS.                     
O|
C|                                   DOCTOR FERRIER                                   
U|                         You can’t trap us here --                                  
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         A medium boasts of having a vision of                      
U|                         murder.  Now she is dead. One of you                       
U|                         felt her eyes upon you -- and killed her.                  
U|                         Tried to kill me. No one shall leave                       
U|                         this place until I know who.                               
U|                         Commissario, stand here and see these                      
U|                         gates do not open. Monsieur Gerard, you                    
U|                         watch the commissario.                                     
O|
C|                                   VITALE PORTFOGLIO                                
U|                         You’d suspect me? I was a cop!                             
O|
C|                                   NICHOLAS                                         
U|                         All the more reason to suspect you.                        
O|
C|                                   OLGA SEMINOFF                                    
U|                         No, they already killed tonight --                         
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         You will all have your chance to speak.                    
O|
N|               He stops.  A stone wall. To Portfoglio --                            
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         Call back the police. Tell them Hercule                    
U|                         Poirot is on the case.                                     
O|
N|               Poirot moves off, into --                                            
O|
S|               INT. BATHROOM. PALAZZO. NIGHT.                                       
O|
N|               Poirot shut the door behind him. His hand on the lock                
N|               TREMBLES.  Alone, the stony mask betrays its cracks.                 
O|
S|               INT. ANTIQUARIUM. PALAZZO. NIGHT.                                    
N|               AT THE BLOODY STATUE OF DEMETER                                      
O|
N|               Reynolds’ body now on a table. Ferrier covers her with a             
N|               sheet.  Ferrier distant, sweating. He looks up -- to --              
O|
S|               INT. BALCONY ABOVE ANTIQUARIUM. PALAZZO.                             
O|
N|               POIROT AT THE JULIET BALCONY                                         
O|
O|
O|                                                                 43.                
O|
N|               Overlooking the high ceiling antiquarium from three floors           
N|               up.  Above Ferrier and Reynolds and Demeter. Cluttered               
N|               storage:  Old paintings leaning in stacks. Ariadne joins.            
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         Is this where she...                                       
O|
N|               She looks over the low balustrade. A shiver.                         
O|
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         It’s one thing to write about murder                       
U|                         another to see it up close.                                
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         The attempt on my life occurred                            
U|                         approximately two minutes after the chime                  
U|                         of midnight. You were first to find the                    
U|                         body at...                                                 
O|
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
P|                             (realizing, a hard stare)                              
U|                         Don’t you dare look at me like a murder                    
U|                         suspect. We’re old friends.                                
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         Every murderer is someone’s old friend.                    
N|               He waits for the ECHO of her SNORT to clear before relenting.        
N|               Moving down the stairs --                                            
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         Alas, you have written too many clever                     
U|                         murders to be caught at the foot of your                   
U|                         first victim. And you are so far viably                    
U|                         alibied by the chef for the time. Which                    
U|                         is why I shall now ask you to assist me                    
U|                         in my investigation.                                       
O|
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
P|                             (delighted)                                            
U|                         When do we start?                                          
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         When you collect for me our host.                          
O|
N|               Poirot takes out A NOTE PAD. A PEN tucked in. CLICK.                 
N|               Ariadne takes him in, enjoying this.                                 
O|
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         Knew you were in there somewhere. All it                   
U|                         took was a corpse and look at you:                         
U|                         Hercule Poirot all over again.                             
O|
O|
O|                                                                 44.                
O|
N,G|               ON ARIADNE. Her smile suddenly falls.  Seeing something. In          
N|               the stack of dusty and cobwebbed covered PAINTINGS.                  
N|               She kneels down. Reaches BEHIND THE STACK -- grasping                
N|               something. Her breath catches as she pulls out:                      
O|
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         Baba the Rabbit.                                           
O|
N|               She shows Poirot. The stuffed rabbit.  Found here. How?              
O|
S|               INT. OUTSIDE ALICIA’S ROOM. PALAZZO. NIGHT.                          
O|
N|               Rowena Drake clutches Baba to her chest. She looks to where          
N|               it was found. Cluttered with art in stacks.                          
C|                                   ROWENA DRAKE                                     
U|                         Baba the Rabbit? Are you sure?  Under                      
U|                         that mess of paintings?...                                 
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         Is this where you last saw Ms. Reynolds?                   
O|
C|                                   ROWENA DRAKE                                     
U|                         Yes.  I begged her to come back tomorrow                   
U|                         night, every night. She refused at                         
U|                         first.  She was too afraid.                                
O|
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         An enemy?                                                  
O|
C|                                   ROWENA DRAKE                                     
U|                         The spirits. She said she felt their                       
U|                         vendetta rising like a migraine. So I                      
U|                         offered her triple. Then I went to Mrs.                    
U|                         Seminoff to decide what to sell to pay                     
U|                         her.  We were in the Music Room.                           
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         When was that?                                             
O|
C|                                   ROWENA DRAKE                                     
U|                         Midnight. Just before.                                     
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         And then you came running.                                 
P|                             (she nods)                                             
U|                         If I may ask, what is directly above this                  
U|                         balcony?                                                   
O|
O|
O|                                                                 45.                
O|
S|               EXT. LOGGIA / ROOFTOP GARDEN. PALAZZO. NIGHT.                        
O|
N,G|               Poirot and Ariadne take in the now empty garden. A VIEW of           
N,G|               Venice aglow under STORM. The RAIN beats a leaky awning over         
N|               BARE FLOWER BEDS and A LINDENWOOD BEEHIVE.                           
O|
C|                                   ROWENA DRAKE                                     
U|                         The garden was our secret hideaway. My                     
U|                         daughter’s favorite place. I let it all                    
U|                         die.                                                       
O|
N|               She kicks over long forgotten rusty gardening tools. She             
N|               steps to the BEEHIVE.                                                
O|
C|                                   ROWENA DRAKE                                     
U|                         Our bees, we made honey. Alicia used to                    
U|                         tease me, “All this effort for a teaspoon                  
U|                         of wildflower honey we can buy for six                     
U|                         lira.”  I held hope they’d survived.                       
P|                             (a hand to it, silent)                                 
U|                         Poor things.                                               
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         How did you come to invite Ms. Reynolds                    
U|                         to your home?                                              
O|
N|               Rowena shivers.                                                      
O|
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         Couldn’t we get out of the rain for this?                  
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         Oh, mais non, let us enjoy the secret                      
U|                         garden.                                                    
O|
C|                                   ROWENA DRAKE                                     
U|                         I read about her in a magazine, I didn’t                   
U|                         think much of it. Then out of the blue                     
U|                         one day she wrote me.                                      
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         A letter from a stranger. But she would                    
U|                         know you from the opera, no?                               
O|
C|                                   ROWENA DRAKE                                     
U|                         It was the name she used. She said she                     
U|                         heard a message ‘For Aspasia.’ That was                    
U|                         Alicia’s pet name for me.                                  
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         Aspasia, the great love of the King of                     
U|                         Pontus.  From “Mitridate.”                                 
O|
O|
O|                                                                 46.                
O|
C|                                   ROWENA DRAKE                                     
U|                         Mozart’s first opera, my first starring                    
U|                         role.  Alicia was born two months before.                  
U|                         I found my voice because of her. Ever                      
U|                         since... I can’t sing without knowing                      
U|                         she’s waiting in my dressing room. I                       
U|                         still can’t.                                               
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         You’ll never perform again?                                
O|
N|               Rowena shakes her head. Spark doused by grief.                       
O|
C|                                   ROWENA DRAKE                                     
U|                         There is no music without her. I turned                    
U|                         down marriage proposals without even a                     
U|                         thought, why would I need anyone else?                     
U|                         She said yes to her first.                                 
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         To the charming chef. Maxime Gerard.                       
O|
C|                                   ROWENA DRAKE                                     
U|                         He’s a pompous ass out to marry the                        
U|                         biggest purse he can charm. When they                      
U|                         got engaged I tore out every flower in                     
U|                         the garden and got on a boat to Istanbul.                  
U|                         He must’ve found I wasn’t as rich as he                    
U|                         thought. The best day of my life was                       
U|                         when he said he met someone else and                       
U|                         called the whole thing off. Then the                       
U|                         worst days started.                                        
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         Her illness.                                               
O|
C|                                   ROWENA DRAKE                                     
U|                         Her mind was on fire. She was like a                       
U|                         little girl again.                                         
O|
N,G|               ON ROWENA. Overcome.  Broken by grief.                               
O|
S|               INT. ALICIA’S ROOM. PALAZZO. NIGHT.                                  
N|               THE BALCONY DOORS RATTLE. Battered by wind.                          
O|
N|               Water streams in from a CRACK in the corner.                         
O|
T|                                                                CUT TO:             
O|
N,G|               CLOSE:  A FORK STABS DOWN --                                         
O|
S|               INT. KITCHEN / PANTRY. PALAZZO. NIGHT.                               
O|
N|               -- Into CAKE. Seminoff with a heaped plate. Nervous eating.          
O|
O|
O|                                                                 47.                
O|
C|                                   OLGA SEMINOFF                                    
U|                         Oh, I’m a terrible housekeeper. I’m all                    
U|                         she could get. A superstitious city.                       
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         But you are superstitious too. This                        
U|                         palazzo you believe is haunted, yes?                       
O|
N|               A pause.  Fork down.                                                 
O|
C|                                   OLGA SEMINOFF                                    
U|                         Ms. Rowena may own it. But the spirits                     
U|                         possess it.                                                
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         And where were you, Madame Seminoff, when                  
U|                         these murderous ghosts turned Joyce                        
U|                         Reynolds into one of their own?                            
N|               Olga pushes aside her plate, hackles raised.                         
O|
C|                                   OLGA SEMINOFF                                    
U|                         Make light, go on. Why do you ask me all                   
U|                         these questions? I did nothing wrong.                      
O|
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         It’s what he does -- or used to do. Back                   
U|                         at it for the moment. It’s going well.                     
O|
C|                                   OLGA SEMINOFF                                    
U|                         What is it that you do?                                    
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         What is it I do? When a crime has been                     
U|                         committed I can, by application of order                   
U|                         and method, and the slow extinguishing of                  
U|                         my own soul, find without fail or                          
U|                         doubt...  whodunit.                                        
O|
N|               Seminoff suddenly delighted.                                         
O|
C|                                   OLGA SEMINOFF                                    
U|                         Like in your books! Your silly detective                   
U|                         from Finland, he’s making lists! Do you                    
U|                         base yourself on her writing?                              
O|
N|               Ariadne’s look is as smug as Poirot’s is melting.                    
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         Could you tell us where were you at                        
U|                         midnight? For the lists.                                   
O|
C|                                   OLGA SEMINOFF                                    
U|                         In the Music Room with Ms. Rowena.                         
O|
O|
O|                                                                 48.                
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         She joined you there before midnight, you                  
U|                         are sure of this?                                          
C|                                   OLGA SEMINOFF                                    
U|                         I was watching the clock and was grateful                  
U|                         that she did.                                              
O|
N|               She looks around her. Shivers.                                       
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         But you did not approve of the fortune                     
U|                         teller.  I believe you called her                          
U|                         “Satanic.” Your mind goes perhaps to                       
U|                         Exodus 22:18.                                              
O|
C|                                   OLGA SEMINOFF                                    
U|                         “Maleficos non patieris vivere.”                           
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         “Do not suffer a sorceress to live.”                       
O|
C|                                   OLGA SEMINOFF                                    
U|                         The Bible warns against it with reason.                    
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         A witch on her Sabbath? To be burned or                    
U|                         drowned -- or thrown off a high balcony?                   
C|                                   OLGA SEMINOFF                                    
U|                         No.  I’m no vigilante.                                     
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         Your Scripture is keen. No less than a                     
U|                         strict Vulgate Latin. Not the product of                   
U|                         a parochial school, but perhaps... a                       
U|                         convent.                                                   
O|
N|               Surprised at his insight. Pride in her past.                         
O|
C|                                   OLGA SEMINOFF                                    
U|                         I felt my call to be a nun before I could                  
U|                         read.  Wore the habit nine years at                        
U|                         Ospedale della Pietà. Then I met Mr.                       
U|                         Seminoff. He came to fix the roof.  God                    
U|                         sets challenges.                                           
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         You fell in love and forgot God.                           
C|                                   OLGA SEMINOFF                                    
U|                         It’s not so simple. Maybe it was.                          
O|
O|
O|                                                                 49.                
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         A final question. For the lists of                         
U|                         course --                                                  
N|               A SOUND interrupts his thought: A WHISPER. Perhaps A SONG.           
N|               Poirot turns --                                                      
O|
N|               But sees NOTHING. He shakes off the confusion. Resumes --            
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         Pardon, Madame... You who fear the dark                    
U|                         arts so. Why would you attend a seance?                    
U|                         An abominated act. Here, at night, when                    
U|                         you are loathe to remain.                                  
O|
N,G|               ON SEMINOFF. As if caught.  As if on the verge of admission.         
O|
C|                                   OLGA SEMINOFF                                    
U|                         There is only one to whom I must answer.                   
U|                         And that is not you.                                       
O|
N,G|               ON POIROT. About to press.  Then HEARING A NEW SOUND...              
N,M|               WATER DRIPPING. He turns -- TIME SLOWS AS HE SEES IT:                
N|               POIROT STANDS BEFORE A WIDE WALL -- CASCADING WITH WATER --          
N|               RIVULETS COMING DOWN FROM THE CEILING --                             
O|
N|               IN THE CLEAR SHAPE OF THE LETTER “M.”                                
O|
C,E|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER (O.S.)                            
P|                             (concerned)                                            
U|                         Poirot?                                                    
O|
N,M,T|               TIME RESUMES: BACK ON POIROT.  LOOKING AT A NORMAL WALL.             
O|
N,G|               ON POIROT. He touches the wall.  Dry.                                
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         Mrs. Olga Seminoff. As a nun, what was                     
U|                         your Saint’s name?                                         
O|
N|               A break in her voice.                                                
O|
C|                                   OLGA SEMINOFF                                    
U|                         Maria.                                                     
O|
N,G|               CLOSE ON:  THE TYPEWRITTEN PAGE. THE LETTER “M.”                     
O|
S|               INT. BATHROOM. PALAZZO. NIGHT.                                       
N|               Poirot at the sink. A stolen moment.  Breath hard to find.           
O|
N|               He holds the page. It shakes in his hand.                            
O|
O|
O|                                                                 50.                
O|
S|               INT. PIANO NOBILE. PALAZZO. NIGHT.                                   
N|               Portfoglio and Maxime at their post by the locked gate.              
N|               Portfoglio alert. Maxime anxious.                                    
O|
N|               Maxime steals a look at the LOCK. As if considering a run.           
N|               Portfoglio catches his eye.                                          
O|
C|                                   VITALE PORTFOGLIO                                
U|                         You won’t get far on a broken leg.                         
O|
N|               Maxime leans against the wall. Not going anywhere.                   
O|
S|               INT. ANTIQUARIUM. PALAZZO. NIGHT.                                    
O|
N|               The DRAPED CARYATIDS supporting the arch stare out. Water            
N|               DRIP-DRIPS onto them.                                                
O|
N,G|               ON REYNOLDS’ BODY. LAID ON THE TABLE. COVERED IN A SHEET,            
N|               BLOOD SOAKED AT THE ABDOMEN.                                         
O|
N|               Doctor Ferrier beside her, finishes his examination.                 
N|               Nervously offers his assessment --                                   
C|                                   DOCTOR FERRIER                                   
U|                         Jagged weapon, not too sharp. Nails                        
U|                         maybe.  No other marks on her. Other                       
U|                         than the obvious.                                          
O|
N|               He accidentally knocks his bag to the floor. He startles.            
N|               Quickly ashamed of his hair trigger. He picks it up.                 
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         Nothing else unusual about her condition?                  
O|
C|                                   DOCTOR FERRIER                                   
U|                         Impaled on Neoclassical art might be                       
U|                         enough.  There’s no such thing as psychic                  
U|                         phenomena if that’s what you’re after.                     
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         What of the wrist? You ignore an injury                    
U|                         as well as the precise time of death.                      
O|
N|               Ferrier looks again. Only now sees the wrist BENT horribly.          
T|               INSERT CUT: THE SHOCK MOMENT OF IMPACT -- REYNOLDS’ ARM              
T|               SMASHES BACK AGAINST MARBLE! THE WRISTWATCH BREAKS! --               
O|
N,G|               CLOSE ON REYNOLDS’ BENT WRIST. Her WATCH. The glass BROKEN.          
N|               Ariadne notes the time on the watch frozen at 12:04.                 
O|
C|                                   DOCTOR FERRIER                                   
U|                         Missed that.                                               
O|
O|
O|                                                                 51.                
O|
N|               Poirot eyes Ferrier. Ferrier’s tics flare under scrutiny.            
C|                                   DOCTOR FERRIER                                   
U|                         You’re staring at me. Stop looking --                      
P|                             (heat building)                                        
U|                         You think I’m a loony? I’m not --                          
O|
N|               Ferrier GRIPS a chair -- brings it BASHING DOWN, when --             
O|
C,E|                                   LEOPOLD (O.S.)                                   
U|                         Dad.  You all right?                                       
O|
N|               LEOPOLD is at the door. Ferrier pulls himself together at            
N|               the sight of his son. The desired effect. Nods.                      
O|
C|                                   LEOPOLD                                          
U|                         Just up here if you need me.                               
O|
N|               Leopold slips out quietly. Ferrier looks off, ashamed.               
O|
C|                                   DOCTOR FERRIER                                   
U|                         Really wish you hadn’t asked me.                           
O|
N,G|               ON POIROT. Softening.  Understanding.                                
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         Battle scars are not always of the body.                   
U|                         You served.                                                
O|
N,G|               ON FERRIER. Eye contact difficult.  Memories individually            
N|               clear, their connections cloudy.                                     
C|                                   DOCTOR FERRIER                                   
U|                         RAMC.  Half a kilometer behind the latest                  
U|                         Wermacht slaughter, patch up boys ripped                   
U|                         apart by MG42s, send them back for more.                   
U|                         Don’t have to fire a rifle to feel like a                  
U|                         murderer every day. Even before we found                   
U|                         the gates.                                                 
O|
N|               The last word a trigger.                                             
O|
C|                                   DOCTOR FERRIER                                   
U|                         15 April ’45. Was all supposed to be                       
U|                         over.  We crossed the Rhine, east in the                   
U|                         push for Berlin. Found the gates.  At                      
U|                         the camp at Bergen-Belsen.                                 
O|
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         Oh, God.                                                   
O|
O|
O|                                                                 52.                
O|
C|                                   DOCTOR FERRIER                                   
U|                         “Liberating.” Nursing skeletons back to                    
U|                         life.  We killed two the first day with                    
U|                         milk.  We didn’t know. Then typhus. All                    
U|                         we had was aspirin and opium. Ferrier                      
U|                         was Fersztenfeld when my grandfather                       
U|                         emigrated from Lodz.                                       
P|                             (then)                                                 
U|                         We burned down the huts. I wrote Leo a                     
U|                         letter and shot myself through the chest.                  
P|                             (then)                                                 
U|                         I was told to stop practicing when I got                   
U|                         home.                                                      
O|
N|               Poirot nods without judgement.                                       
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         Except for one patient. Last year.                         
U|                         Alicia Drake.                                              
O|
C|                                   DOCTOR FERRIER                                   
U|                         A favor to Rowena. No other doctor would                   
U|                         see her, not here. I’d been her family                     
U|                         doctor so long. Should have said no.                       
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         You were at a disadvantage there... You                    
U|                         are in love with Rowena Drake.                             
O|
N|               Ferrier hesitates... But submits.  Long resigned.                    
O|
C|                                   DOCTOR FERRIER                                   
U|                         Lucky to be in her life at all.                            
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         I know this has been --                                    
O|
N|               Poirot pauses. Tripping on the word.  On his thoughts.               
N|               Ariadne’s concern cut short as he reclaims focus.                    
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         I know this has been difficult, but in                     
U|                         your opinion... could Alicia Drake have                    
U|                         been murdered?                                             
O|
N,G|               ON FERRIER. No easy answer.  Not when he is honest.                  
C|                                   DOCTOR FERRIER                                   
U|                         Alicia told me what she saw. Said the                      
U|                         children were taunting her. I didn’t                       
U|                         listen.  Wrote it off as a broken heart --                 
U|                         when she was going mad. She needed help,                   
U|                         I gave her sedatives. Like milk to the                     
U|                         starving.                                                  
O|                                   (MORE)                                           
O|
O|
O|                                                                 53.                
O|
