U|                         ashamed at how much he likes attention.                    
U|                         I know something of this.                                  
O|                                   (MORE)                                           
O|
O|
O|                                                                 95.                
O|
C,E|                                   POIROT (cont'd)                                  
U|                         You must never blame yourself for                          
U|                         anything that happened in this house.                      
C|                                   OLGA SEMINOFF                                    
U|                         Why would he do that?                                      
O|
N|               But Poirot and Leopold do understand one another. Leopold            
N|               for once looks like a normal boy. Small. Guilty.                     
O|
C|                                   LEOPOLD                                          
U|                         But it’s all my fault.                                     
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         You didn’t care for money for yourself...                  
U|                         You wanted to help your father.                            
O|
C|                                   LEOPOLD                                          
U|                         Dad couldn’t work, I only used the money                   
U|                         for our bills. I didn’t even know what                     
U|                         to do with the rest. It’s all under my                     
U|                         mattress.                                                  
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         Rowena Drake presumed her blackmailer was                  
U|                         the fake medium or the family doctor.                      
U|                         When it was neither. Only the doctor’s                     
U|                         son saw the truth.                                         
C|                                   LEOPOLD                                          
U|                         It was obvious from Dad’s notes. Mad                       
U|                         honey poisoning, just like Mitridate from                  
U|                         her opera. “The Poison King.”  I read                      
U|                         all about it in one of her books in the                    
U|                         library. I tested the theory...                            
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         You sent a blackmail letter. Another...                    
O|
N|               Leopold nods, ashamed.                                               
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         Perhaps there is a use for this money in                   
U|                         your mattress. To make good of regret.                     
O|
S|               EXT. CAMPO. PALAZZO LACRIME DI GIOVANI. DAWN.                        
O|
N|               NICHOLAS AND DESDEMONA                                               
N|               Await their fate and future with trepidation.                        
O|
C|                                   NICHOLAS                                         
U|                         We survived before Ms. Reynolds. We’ll                     
U|                         do better than survive. I promised we’d                    
U|                         get to America and we will.                                
O|
O|
O|                                                                 96.                
O|
N|               She feels the hollowness of optimism. At which, Seminoff and         
N|               Leopold approach, resolute. Their boat waiting for them.             
C|                                   OLGA SEMINOFF                                    
U|                         Come with us you two.                                      
O|
C|                                   NICHOLAS                                         
U|                         To the police station?                                     
O|
C|                                   OLGA SEMINOFF                                    
U|                         Us to home. You, to America.  Missouri I                   
U|                         understand. Passage for two.  We can                       
U|                         help with that I think.                                    
O|
N,G|               ON DESDEMONA. She lights up with stolen hope.                        
O|
N|               She looks to Nicholas. Takes his hand, and --                        
O|
N|               They step on the boat with Seminoff. A journey begun.                
O|
N|               Leopold joins them. A final word to Poirot --                        
O|
C|                                   LEOPOLD                                          
U|                         You solved the case. But you had help,                     
U|                         didn’t you? You heard her.                                 
O|
N|               A pause.  Poirot’s thoughts his own.                                 
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         Bonne chance, my friend.                                   
O|
C|                                   LEOPOLD                                          
U|                         Don’t worry, people who die in this house                  
U|                         always come back. I’ll see them again.                     
P|                             (waves to the palazzo)                                 
U|                         I’ll see you soon, Dad.                                    
O|
N|               And their boat motors away. A spark of possibility restored.         
O|
N|               Poirot watches them disappear up the canal.                          
O|
N|               ON THE BRIDGE                                                        
O|
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         I won’t apologize. You ruined my book.                     
U|                         Neither living nor proof. Have to                          
U|                         rewrite the whole thing.                                   
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         So long as it does not include my name.                    
O|
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         I never want to hear your name again.                      
O|
O|
O|                                                                 97.                
O|
N|               She notes Poirot staring up reverentially at the palazzo.            
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         Just a house now, all debunked... But                      
U|                         you’ve got the look like a believer.                       
P|                             (off Poirot, realizing)                                
U|                         You did.  You saw something.                               
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         I was under the influence. My                              
U|                         subconscious mind assembled facts ahead                    
U|                         of the rational --                                         
O|
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         You saw.  You know.                                        
O|
N|               A pause.                                                             
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         I know only we cannot hide from our                        
U|                         ghosts.  Whether they are real or not.                     
U|                         We must make our peace with them. And                      
U|                         live life somehow.                                         
O|
C|                                   ARIADNE OLIVER                                   
U|                         And how will you live your life?                           
O|
N,G|               CLOSE ON POIROT. The question labors in his mind. And --             
S|               EXT. VENICE STREET / RECTORY. DAY.                                   
O|
N|               Weary Poirot nears his front gate. The morning’s box of              
N|               pastries left untouched. The familiar PETITIONERS asleep             
N|               outside.  Still waiting.                                             
O|
N|               Poirot walks right by Desperate Petitioner.                          
O|
N|               Who wakes up abruptly. Looks up to see --                            
O|
N|               THE RECTORY GATE LEFT OPEN BEHIND POIROT.                            
O|
N|               THE CHURCH BELL STRIKES. Petitioner blinks. Fixes his hair           
N|               and tie, and --                                                      
O|
S|               EXT. ROOF TERRACE. DAY.                                              
O|
N|               FOLLOW PETITIONER. Tentatively entering.                             
O|
C|                                   DESPERATE PETITIONER                             
U|                         Monsieur? Monsieur Poirot?                                 
O|
N|               Petitioner comes up the stairs to find --                            
O|
O|
O|                                                                 98.                
O|
N|               POIROT.  Eyes alert despite the sleepless night. Ignoring            
N|               the city view to scan through documents and make his notes.          
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         You parents died one soon after the                        
U|                         other, then your brother.                                  
O|
N|               Petitioner nods.                                                     
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         Your trusted doctor is a beloved old                       
U|                         friend to the family.                                      
O|
N|               Petitioner nods.                                                     
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         Your brother was unmarried, as are you.                    
U|                         No other relations.                                        
O|
N|               Petitioner nods. Poirot circling as --                               
O|
C|                                   POIROT                                           
U|                         Your family is not cursed. I suspect                       
U|                         your brother added a codicil to his will                   
U|                         naming your doctor beneficiary should all                  
U|                         the members of your small family perish,                   
U|                         after which I suspect he then murdered                     
U|                         them claiming natural causes at their                      
U|                         deathbeds -- and your life is next in                      
U|                         danger.  You may wish to sit --                            
N|               Poirot stops suddenly. FROZEN.  Seeing it:                           
O|
N|               A LILAC TEACUP                                                       
O|
N|               On the edge of a table. Just off.                                    
O|
N|               Poirot stares at it a long time.                                     
O|
N|               Pushes it back. Safely on.                                           
O|
N|               Listening.                                                           
O|
M|               END.                                                                 
